### What are all the Spring bean scopes?

Spring provides six built-in scopes. The first two work in any application. The remaining four require a web-aware `ApplicationContext`.

| Scope | Instances | Lifecycle | Destruction |
|---|---|---|---|
| `singleton` | One per ApplicationContext | Created at startup (eager) | @PreDestroy on shutdown |
| `prototype` | New per getBean/injection | Created on demand | NOT managed by Spring |
| `request` | One per HTTP request | Created on first access | Destroyed after response |
| `session` | One per HTTP session | Created on first access | Destroyed on session expiry |
| `application` | One per ServletContext | Created on first access | Destroyed on context shutdown |
| `websocket` | One per WebSocket session | Created on first access | Destroyed on session close |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Scope;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.util.UUID;

// SINGLETON (default) -- one instance, shared by all
@Component
public class InterestRateService {
    // All threads share this instance
}

// PROTOTYPE -- new instance every time
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class PaymentTransaction {
    private final String transactionId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();

    public String getTransactionId() {
        return transactionId;
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank;

import com.bank.service.PaymentTransaction;
import org.springframework.context.ApplicationContext;

public class ScopeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ApplicationContext ctx = // ...
        PaymentTransaction t1 = ctx.getBean(PaymentTransaction.class);
        PaymentTransaction t2 = ctx.getBean(PaymentTransaction.class);
        System.out.println(t1.getTransactionId()); // e.g., a3f2-...
        System.out.println(t2.getTransactionId()); // e.g., b7c4-...
        System.out.println(t1 == t2); // false -- different instances
    }
}
```

```text
a3f2e4d1-1234-4567-89ab-cdef01234567
b7c4a8e2-9876-5432-10fe-dcba98765432
false
```

### What is the difference between singleton and prototype?

| Aspect | Singleton | Prototype |
|---|---|---|
| Instances | One per container | New per request |
| Initialization | Eager (at startup) | Lazy (on demand) |
| Thread safety | Your responsibility | Not shared, inherently safe |
| @PreDestroy | Runs on shutdown | Never runs |
| Memory | One object in heap | Many objects, GC reclaims |
| Use case | Stateless services | Stateful per-operation objects |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.context.ApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service // singleton by default
public class TransferService {
    @Autowired
    private ApplicationContext ctx;

    public void transfer(String from, String to, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        // Get a fresh prototype for each transfer
        PaymentTransaction tx = ctx.getBean(PaymentTransaction.class);
        System.out.println("Transaction: " + tx.getTransactionId());
        // Each call gets a NEW PaymentTransaction
    }
}
```

### What is the prototype-into-singleton trap?

When you inject a prototype-scoped bean directly into a singleton, the singleton's constructor runs ONCE. The prototype is resolved ONCE during that construction and stored in a `final` field forever. Every subsequent use of the singleton sees the same prototype instance, defeating the purpose of prototype scope.

**WRONG -- direct injection of prototype into singleton:**

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class PaymentProcessingService {
    private final PaymentTransaction transaction; // injected ONCE

    public PaymentProcessingService(PaymentTransaction transaction) {
        this.transaction = transaction; // this ONE instance is stored forever
    }

    public void processPayment(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // WRONG: every payment uses the SAME transaction object
        System.out.println("Processing with TX: " + transaction.getTransactionId());
        // Always prints the same ID!
    }
}
```

```text
processPayment("SAV2001", 10000) -> Processing with TX: a3f2-...
processPayment("SAV2002", 20000) -> Processing with TX: a3f2-...  // SAME ID!
processPayment("SAV2003", 30000) -> Processing with TX: a3f2-...  // SAME ID!
```

### How do you fix the prototype-into-singleton trap?

There are three fixes, from most preferred to least:

**Fix 1: ObjectProvider (PREFERRED)**

`ObjectProvider<T>` is a lazy, factory-like wrapper. Call `getObject()` each time you need a fresh prototype. This is the cleanest and most explicit approach.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.ObjectProvider;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class PaymentProcessingService {
    private final ObjectProvider<PaymentTransaction> transactionProvider;

    public PaymentProcessingService(
            ObjectProvider<PaymentTransaction> transactionProvider) {
        this.transactionProvider = transactionProvider;
    }

    public void processPayment(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // getObject() creates a FRESH prototype each time
        PaymentTransaction tx = transactionProvider.getObject();
        System.out.println("Processing with TX: " + tx.getTransactionId());
    }
}
```

```text
processPayment("SAV2001", 10000) -> Processing with TX: a3f2-...
processPayment("SAV2002", 20000) -> Processing with TX: b7c4-...  // different!
processPayment("SAV2003", 30000) -> Processing with TX: c9d8-...  // different!
```

**Fix 2: @Lookup method**

Spring overrides an abstract method (or a concrete method you annotate with `@Lookup`) with a `getBean()` call. This works but is less explicit than `ObjectProvider`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Lookup;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public abstract class PaymentProcessingService {

    @Lookup
    protected abstract PaymentTransaction createTransaction();

    public void processPayment(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        PaymentTransaction tx = createTransaction(); // fresh prototype
        System.out.println("Processing with TX: " + tx.getTransactionId());
    }
}
```

The downside is that the class must be abstract (or the method overridden), which makes unit testing harder because you cannot create the service with `new`.

**Fix 3: Scoped proxy**

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Scope;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.ScopedProxyMode;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.util.UUID;

@Component
@Scope(value = "prototype", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)
public class PaymentTransaction {
    private final String transactionId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();

    public String getTransactionId() {
        return transactionId;
    }
}
```

With `proxyMode = TARGET_CLASS`, Spring injects a CGLIB proxy. Every method call on the proxy delegates to a fresh prototype instance. The singleton never holds the real bean, only the proxy.

### How does request scope work?

Request-scoped beans are created once per HTTP request and destroyed after the response is sent. They are useful for holding per-request data like trace IDs and authenticated user information.

```java
package com.bank.context;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.context.annotation.RequestScope;

@Component
@RequestScope
public class RequestContext {
    private String traceId;
    private String authenticatedUser;

    public String getTraceId() { return traceId; }
    public void setTraceId(String traceId) { this.traceId = traceId; }
    public String getAuthenticatedUser() { return authenticatedUser; }
    public void setAuthenticatedUser(String user) { this.authenticatedUser = user; }
}
```

When a singleton injects a request-scoped bean, Spring automatically creates a scoped proxy. The singleton holds the proxy, and each method call delegates to the RequestContext for the current HTTP request.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.context.RequestContext;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class AuditService {
    private final RequestContext requestContext; // scoped proxy

    public AuditService(RequestContext requestContext) {
        this.requestContext = requestContext;
    }

    public void logAction(String action) {
        // Each HTTP request gets its own RequestContext
        System.out.println("[" + requestContext.getTraceId() + "] "
            + requestContext.getAuthenticatedUser() + ": " + action);
    }
}
```

```text
// Request 1 (Rajesh): [trace-001] Rajesh: Transfer 10000 to SAV2002
// Request 2 (Priya):  [trace-002] Priya: Check balance SAV2003
```

### What are edge cases with bean scopes?

- **Prototype beans and @PreDestroy.** Spring does not track prototypes after creation. `@PreDestroy` never runs. If the prototype holds resources (file handles, connections), you must close them manually.
- **Session scope in REST APIs.** REST APIs are typically stateless with no HTTP session. Session-scoped beans are useless unless you enable sessions explicitly.
- **Request scope outside a web request.** Accessing a request-scoped bean from a background thread (e.g., `@Async`) throws `IllegalStateException: No thread-bound request found`. Pass the data as method parameters instead.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class NotificationService {
    // WRONG: request-scoped bean not available in @Async thread
    // private final RequestContext requestContext;

    @Async
    public void sendNotification(String traceId, String userId, String message) {
        // CORRECT: pass request data as parameters
        System.out.println("[" + traceId + "] Sending to " + userId + ": " + message);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with bean scopes?

**WRONG -- assuming prototype beans are destroyed:**

```java
package com.bank.service;

import jakarta.annotation.PreDestroy;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Scope;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class ReportGenerator {
    private java.io.FileWriter writer;

    public void generate(String reportPath) throws Exception {
        writer = new java.io.FileWriter(reportPath);
        writer.write("Account report...");
    }

    @PreDestroy // NEVER CALLED for prototypes!
    public void cleanup() throws Exception {
        if (writer != null) writer.close();
    }
}
```

**CORRECT -- close resources manually or use try-with-resources:**

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Scope;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.io.FileWriter;

@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class ReportGenerator {

    public void generate(String reportPath) throws Exception {
        try (FileWriter writer = new FileWriter(reportPath)) {
            writer.write("Account report for National Bank of India");
        } // auto-closed here
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **ObjectProvider vs @Lookup.** `ObjectProvider` is preferred because it keeps the class concrete and testable. `@Lookup` requires the class to be abstract or non-final, making unit testing harder.
- **Custom scopes.** You can create custom scopes by implementing `Scope` and registering it. Examples: tenant scope for multi-tenant SaaS, conversation scope for wizard flows.
- **Refresh scope (Spring Cloud).** `@RefreshScope` creates a proxy that recreates the bean when `/actuator/refresh` is called, useful for dynamic configuration changes without restart.
- **Thread scope.** There is no built-in thread scope, but `SimpleThreadScope` exists for testing. In production, use `ThreadLocal` or request-scoped beans.
- **Prototype performance.** Creating a new object per call has overhead. For high-throughput paths, consider object pooling (Apache Commons Pool) instead of prototype scope.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What happens if you inject a prototype into a singleton? (ONE prototype forever.)
- Does `@PreDestroy` run for prototype beans? (No.)
- How does `ObjectProvider` work internally? (It wraps a `BeanFactory.getBean()` call.)
- Can you use request scope outside of a web application? (No, throws `IllegalStateException`.)
- What is the difference between `@RequestScope` and `@Scope("request")`? (`@RequestScope` is a composed annotation that includes `proxyMode = TARGET_CLASS` by default.)
